{
  "case_id": "2008-08-267",
  "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Court of Appeals for the Armed Forces, or CAAF, held that military appellate courts possess open-ended jurisdiction under the All Writs Act to entertain a coram nobis challenge to the merits of a final court-martial conviction. This Court should reverse that decision, both because the All Writs Act cannot supply jurisdiction that Congress chose not to confer and because Respondent is a civilian who may no longer invoke the military court system."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First, as noted in Goldsmith, the UCMJ narrowly circumscribes military appellate jurisdiction to the findings and sentences of a court-martial conviction. That is a direct review jurisdiction. UCMJ Articles 66 and 67, which define appellate jurisdiction, do not contemplate any further review within the military appellate courts."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Shah, how does that differ from the general rule that a judgment becomes final and has preclusive effect once the appellate route has been exhausted or the time to pursue it has expired?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What was the occasion for it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0000-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 16,
        "start": 16
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The All Writs Act does not confer jurisdiction. The Court made that very clear in Goldsmith. What it requires is an independent basis of jurisdiction. That exists in Article III courts. It does not exist in the military court system."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 0.6,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0001-0001",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "No military soldier can correct an error, no matter how egregious, even a technical -- you know, they just wrote the wrong thing down, because Congress didn't want them to."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Freedus",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Because there are courts, appellate military courts must have coram nobis power to protect the integrity of their judgments. The court of appeals correctly asserted the same coram nobis power that Article III courts have."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What do you mean, \"they must\"? Do you think it's unconstitutional to deprive them of that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Freedus",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think Congress has the authority to legislate very broadly in the area of collateral remedies, and Congress could take away the writ of coram nobis if it left in its place an adequate and effective substitute, like it did in 2255."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Freedus",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0001-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Yes. I would like to -- yes, Your Honor, it has been used since 1966. I assume you may be asking about before 1950."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Will you clarify something for me. Are you contending that the result you seek is constitutionally compelled?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: But was -- that question wasn't resolved, was it? I thought we were just talking about the authority of the military courts to issue this writ, and the question that Justice Breyer has raised, well, is this ineffective assistance of counsel adequate grounds to issue the writ? I thought that question was certainly not raised before this Court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "United States v. Kwan and United States v. Castro are two court -- two cases that give the answer to your question and both of those indicate that ineffective assistance of counsel in very similar factual circumstances to this is a basis for coram nobis relief after -- after the ineffective assistance is discovered."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Do I understand correctly that since 1989 there were a total of 30 coram nobis petitions filed, and of those only 4 were granted?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: Where are -- where are the procedures set out for -- for military habeas?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Freedus",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0002-0007",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In Noyd v. Bond this Court squarely held that military -- the Court of Appeals at the time has habeas authority."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Mr. Shah, could you tell us what the government's position is on whether, when somebody has been discharged from the service and then a conviction which he -- which he suffered while he was in the service is set aside, can he be retried in military courts?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Shah (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SHAH: Not if he has passed his enlistment period, no, Your Honor. The government's view is they would not be able to retry him."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: What has direct review got to do with habeas?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Shah (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2008-08-267",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dfee6d22d9f1f5e78b067ccae1581c7d525e8182b53deb9937581ec94685df13",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dfee6d22d9f1-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SHAH: Well -- well, Your Honor, the -- the habeas would be in aid of the direct review jurisdiction. In -- in Noyd -- in the Noyd v. Bond --"
    },
    {
